<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.4: Interpreting Graphs of Quadratic Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01837</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.4: Interpreting Graphs of Quadratic Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7e8a5f1d-6918-495c-8505-9f46cef62d2d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para>The height in feet, \(h\), of a stomp rocket (propelled by a short blast of air) above the ground after \(t\) seconds is given by the equation \(h(t)=5+100t-16t^2\). Here is a graph that represents \(h\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Graph of the quadratic function h(t)=5+100t−16t2 on a coordinate plane, origin O . Horizontal axis scale 0 to 7 by 1’s, labeled “time (seconds)”. Vertical axis scale 0 to 200 by 50’s, labeled “height above ground (feet)”. Some of the points are (0 comma 5), (1 comma 89), (2 comma 141), (3 comma 161) to a maximum near (3 point 125 comma 161 point 25), then decreasing through (4 comma 149), (5 comma 105), (6 comma 29) and (6.3 comma 0).">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_08_09_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How does the 5 in the equation relate to the graph?</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The graph intersects the vertical axis at 5.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does \(100t\) in the equation mean in terms of the rocket?</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>It indicates that the initial velocity of the rocket was 100 feet per second upward.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does the \(-16t^2\) mean in terms of the rocket?</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>This indicates the effect of gravity pulling the rocket back toward Earth.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> About when does the rocket hit the ground?</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>At about 6.3 seconds.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
